<section class="section" style="padding-top: 0;">
  <div class="wrap">

    <!-- Step 1 -->
    <div class="step" id="stdio">
      <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">1</span><h2>Run it locally with Claude Code</h2></div>
      <p>With Node 26+, which runs the TypeScript directly, clone the repo and register it as a stdio server. Point
        <code>RAGDOWN_DOCS_DIR</code> at your Markdown folder — it is the only required setting:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code>git clone {{ site.repo }} &amp;&amp; cd mcp-ragdown
npm install
claude mcp add ragdown -e RAGDOWN_DOCS_DIR=$HOME/notes -- node $PWD/src/cli.ts stdio</code></pre>
      <p>On first start the default embedder (about 50 MB) downloads once, then the folder is indexed into
        <code>~/.cache/ragdown/</code>. Claude now has <code>ragdown_recall</code> and <code>ragdown_read_doc</code>,
        and calls them itself when the notes might help. Ask it something your notes answer.</p>
      <p class="callout"><strong>Several windows, one index.</strong> Open as many Claude Code sessions on the same
        folder as you like: the first process becomes the primary and indexes and watches; the rest are readers that
        search the same index.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 2 -->
    <div class="step" id="docker">
      <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">2</span><h2>Run the Docker image with a token</h2></div>
      <p>To share the notes with other machines or agents, run <code>serve</code>, which the image does by default.
        It has the default model baked in, so it starts offline. Save this as <code>docker-compose.yml</code>:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code>services:
  ragdown:
    image: {{ site.dockerImage }}:latest
    ports:
      - '3300:3000'
    volumes:
      - ~/notes:/docs          <span class="cmt"># add :ro and RAGDOWN_READ_ONLY=true to forbid writes</span>
      - ragdown-data:/data     <span class="cmt"># the index, so restarts only diff</span>
    environment:
      RAGDOWN_TOKEN: ${RAGDOWN_TOKEN}
    restart: unless-stopped

volumes:
  ragdown-data:</code></pre>
      <pre class="code"><code>export RAGDOWN_TOKEN=$(openssl rand -hex 32)   <span class="cmt"># keep it; clients need it too</span>
docker compose up -d
curl localhost:3300/api/status                  <span class="cmt"># "ready": true, and the file and chunk counts</span></code></pre>
      <p>Without Docker, the same server is
        <code>RAGDOWN_DOCS_DIR=~/notes RAGDOWN_TOKEN=… node src/cli.ts serve</code> after <code>npm run build</code>
        (which builds the web UI).</p>
      <p class="callout"><strong>No token?</strong> <code>serve</code> refuses to start without
        <code>RAGDOWN_TOKEN</code> unless <code>SECURE_LOCAL_NET=true</code>, which skips auth for a trusted
        network. The first index of a large folder takes minutes; searches answer from what is indexed so far.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 3 -->
    <div class="step" id="connect">
      <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">3</span><h2>Connect a client over HTTP</h2></div>
      <p>Point Claude Code — or any streamable-HTTP MCP client — at <code>/mcp</code> with the token:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code>claude mcp add --transport http ragdown http://localhost:3300/mcp \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer $RAGDOWN_TOKEN"</code></pre>
      <p>The same six tools as over stdio: search, read, stats, and — unless read-only — <code>ragdown_remember</code>
        to save a note and <code>ragdown_reindex</code> to sync on demand.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 4 -->
    <div class="step" id="web-ui">
      <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">4</span><h2>Open the web UI</h2></div>
      <p>Visit <code>http://localhost:3300</code> and paste the token once; the browser keeps it. The
        <strong>Documents</strong> page lists every indexed file. Filter by title or path, and click one to read it
        rendered beside the list, with its size, line count and how many chunks it became.</p>
      <figure class="shot">
        <div class="browser">
          <div class="browser-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><em>localhost:3300/?doc=runbooks/backups.md</em></div>
          <img class="zoomable" src="{{ '/assets/screenshots/documents.png' | url }}"
            alt="The Documents page with runbooks/backups.md open in the preview"
            loading="lazy" width="1440" height="900" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>The sidebar shows the last sync, the file and chunk counts, and the embedder in use.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 5 -->
    <div class="step" id="manage">
      <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">5</span><h2>Upload and delete notes</h2></div>
      <p>The upload button above the list opens <strong>Upload Markdown</strong>. Pick <code>.md</code>,
        <code>.markdown</code> or <code>.mdx</code> files, one pick at a time, and optionally a folder to put them
        under — missing folders are created. The upload returns once the files are indexed. A file that already
        exists is not replaced unasked: it stays in the list with an <strong>Overwrite</strong> button.</p>
      <figure class="shot">
        <div class="browser">
          <div class="browser-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><em>Upload Markdown</em></div>
          <img class="zoomable" src="{{ '/assets/screenshots/upload.png' | url }}"
            alt="The Upload Markdown dialog with the folder notes/reviews and one picked file"
            loading="lazy" width="1440" height="900" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Uploads go into the docs folder itself, so they are ordinary Markdown files from then on.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>The trash button on a previewed file deletes it from the docs folder — not only from the index — after a
        confirmation.</p>
      <figure class="shot">
        <div class="browser">
          <div class="browser-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><em>Delete this document?</em></div>
          <img class="zoomable" src="{{ '/assets/screenshots/delete-confirm.png' | url }}"
            alt="The confirmation shown before deleting runbooks/backups.md"
            loading="lazy" width="1440" height="900" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Under <code>RAGDOWN_READ_ONLY=true</code> both upload and delete are refused.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 6 -->
    <div class="step" id="settings">
      <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">6</span><h2>Check the settings</h2></div>
      <p>The <strong>Settings</strong> page holds this browser's theme and stored token, and shows how the server is
        set up: the docs folder, embedder, role, read-only and watch flags, the last sync, and the search defaults a
        hook gets. Server values come from environment variables at start, so change one and restart to apply it.</p>
      <figure class="shot">
        <div class="browser">
          <div class="browser-bar"><span></span><span></span><span></span><em>localhost:3300/settings</em></div>
          <img class="zoomable" src="{{ '/assets/screenshots/settings.png' | url }}"
            alt="The Settings page: appearance, access, the index card and the search defaults"
            loading="lazy" width="1440" height="1660" />
        </div>
        <figcaption>Each server value names the environment variable it comes from.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 7 -->
    <div class="step" id="scopes">
      <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">7</span><h2>Give each agent one folder</h2></div>
      <p><code>/mcp/&lt;folder&gt;</code> serves the same tools with that folder as the root: searches stay inside it,
        paths are relative to it, and <code>ragdown_remember</code> writes into it. Any folder the indexer walks
        works, with nothing to configure:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code>claude mcp add --transport http notes-alpha http://localhost:3300/mcp/projects/alpha \
  --header "Authorization: Bearer $RAGDOWN_TOKEN"</code></pre>
      <p class="callout"><strong>Focused, not fenced.</strong> A scope keeps an agent on its project's notes, but the
        token that opens <code>/mcp/projects/alpha</code> also opens <code>/mcp</code>.</p>
    </div>

    <!-- Step 8 -->
    <div class="step" id="hook">
      <div class="step-head"><span class="step-num">8</span><h2>Add notes to every turn with a hook</h2></div>
      <p>For context without the model having to ask, a hook calls <code>ragdown_context</code> before each turn.
        It is an MCP tool like the others, so it needs a client whose hooks call MCP tools — min-agent does. There is no
        hook command to wire into Claude Code; there, Claude calls <code>ragdown_recall</code> itself. In min-agent, add the server under <strong>Settings → MCP</strong>:</p>
      <pre class="code"><code>{
  "id": "ragdown",
  "label": "Notes",
  "transport": "http",
  "url": "http://localhost:3300/mcp",
  "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer &lt;RAGDOWN_TOKEN&gt;" },
  "hiddenTools": ["ragdown_context"],
  "hooks": [
    { "id": "notes", "on": "beforeTurn", "tool": "ragdown_context", "inject": true, "maxTokens": 800,
      "args": { "prompt": "{% raw %}{{prompt}}{% endraw %}", "session_id": "min-agent:{% raw %}{{session.id}}{% endraw %}", "max_chars": 3000 } }
  ]
}</code></pre>
      <p>The hook sends the user's message. <code>ragdown_context</code> returns the sections that clear the
        similarity bar, wrapped in <code>&lt;ragdown-context&gt;</code>, or empty text when nothing fits. It skips short
        prompts and slash commands, and never returns the same section twice in one session, so a long chat pays for
        each note once. <code>hiddenTools</code> keeps the hook's tool away from the model, which has
        <code>ragdown_recall</code>.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="step" style="text-align: center;">
      <h2 style="margin: 0 0 10px;">That's it — your notes are in the loop.</h2>
      <p style="margin: 0 auto 22px; max-width: 60ch;">Keep writing Markdown; the index follows. For every tool,
        setting and route — and how to pick an embedder — read the docs.</p>
      <div class="cta-row" style="justify-content: center;">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="{{ '/docs/' | url }}">Read the docs</a>
        <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="{{ site.repo }}">GitHub ↗</a>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>
